-- title:
--   The stabilizer group $\mathcal S$
-- statement:
--   The stabilizer group of the code is the group generated by the $2(n-1)$ generators of (12),
--
--   $$
--   \mathcal S = \Big\langle \prod_{i=1}^n X_{j,i}X_{j+1,i},\ \prod_{i=1}^n Z_{i,j}Z_{i,j+1},\ \forall j\in\mathbb Z_{n-1}\Big\rangle ,
--   $$
--
--   taken modulo phase. In the paper's words its elements are "made up of an even number of rows consisting entirely of $X$ operators and an even number of columns consisting entirely of $Z$ operators".
--
--   **Formalization Note** Modulo phase, multiplying Pauli operators adds their pairs of bit matrices, so $\mathcal S$ is the $\mathbb Z_2$-span of the generator pairs. The paper also displays $\mathcal S$ as the set (10). Read literally, (10)'s condition $\bigoplus_i(\bigwedge_j a_{i,j}) = 0$ also admits operators whose rows are not full (for $n=2$, a single $X_{1,1}$), and such operators anticommute with elements of $\mathcal T$, contrary to p.4. This definition follows (11); the milestone `BaconOQEC.S_props` proves that it equals the prose description.
-- source:
--   D. Bacon, Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories, arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.A, eqs. (10)–(12), pp.4–5

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_SX
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_SZ

namespace BaconOQEC

/-- Bacon, *Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories*,
arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.A, eq. (11), p.4: the stabilizer group
"S = ⟨∏_{i=1}^n X_{j,i}X_{j+1,i}, ∏_{i=1}^n Z_{i,j}Z_{i,j+1}, ∀j ∈ Z_{n−1}⟩", generated by the
`S^X_j` and `S^Z_j` of (12), modulo phase: the `Z₂`-span of the generator pairs `SX j`, `SZ j`.

**Formalization Note.** Multiplication of Pauli operators modulo phase is addition of the pairs
`(a, b)` of bit matrices, so the generated group is the `ZMod 2`-submodule spanned by the
generators. (Products of `X`-type and `Z`-type generators carry no phase beyond `±1`.) The paper
also writes `S` as the set (10); taken literally, (10)'s condition `⊕_i (∧_j a_{i,j}) = 0` admits
operators whose rows are not full (for `n = 2`, a single `X_{1,1}`), which anticommute with
elements of `T`, contrary to p.4. The prose before (10) ("an even number of rows consisting
entirely of X operators and an even number of columns consisting entirely of Z operators") and the
generators (11) agree; this definition is (11), and milestone `BaconOQEC.S_props` proves that it
equals the prose set. -/
def SGroup (m : ℕ) : Submodule (ZMod 2) (Bits m × Bits m) :=
  Submodule.span (ZMod 2) (Set.range (SX (m := m)) ∪ Set.range (SZ (m := m)))

end BaconOQEC


